

<exercise>
<introduction>
<p>
Determine whether each equation has one solution, no solutions, or an infinite number of solutions. If there is a solution, find it.
</p>
</introduction>

<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>3(x-1)=-3(x+1)</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Distributing on each side,
<md>
3x-3 = -3x-3.
</md> Adding <m>3</m> to both sides,
<md>
3x=-3x.
</md> Adding <m>3x</m> to both sides,
<md>
6x=0.
</md> Therefore <m>x=0</m> is the only solution.
</p>
</solution>
</task>



<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>6x+5=-4</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Subtracting <m>5</m> from both sides,
<md>
6x=-9.
</md> Dividing both sides by <m>6</m>,
<md>
x = -\frac{9}{6} = -\frac{3}{2}
</md> is the only solution.
</p>
</solution>
</task>



<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>5x+7=5(x+1)+1</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Distributing on the right side,
<md>
5x+7 = 5x+5+1 .
</md> Therefore we have
<md>
5x+7 = 5x+6.
</md> Subtracting <m>5x</m> from both sides,
<md>
7=6.
</md> Clearly impossible!! So there is no solution.
</p>
</solution>
</task>


<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>-2x+3=-2(x-1)+1</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Distributing on the right side,
<md>
-2x+3 = -2x+2+1.
</md> Therefore we have
<md>
-2x +3 = -2x+3.
</md> Adding <m>2x</m> to both sides,
<md>
3=3.
</md> Clearly always true!! Therefore there are infinitely many solutions.
</p>
</solution>
</task>



</exercise>


<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
Solve the equation <m>g(t)=5</m> where <m>g(t)=\dfrac{-7}{6-2t}</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
We set up
<md>
5 = \frac{-7}{6-2t}.
</md> Multiplying both sides by <m>6-2t</m>, then
<md>
5(6-2t) = -7.
</md> Distributing on the left,
<md>
30 - 10t = -7
</md> and then subtracting <m>30</m> from both sides,
<md>
-10t = -37 \quad \implies \quad t = \frac{-37}{-10}  = 3.7.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>



<exercise>
<introduction>
<p>
Is each line horizontal, vertical, or neither? If it is horizontal or vertical, write an equation for the line and graph the line.
</p>
</introduction>

<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
The line through <m>(0,6)</m> and <m>(1,6)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The slope is given by
<md>
m = \frac{6-6}{1-0}  = \frac{0}{1}=0.
</md> Therefore the line is horizontal, and further the vertical intercept was given as <m>b=6</m> so <m>y=6</m> is the equation. The graph is below.
</p>

\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
\begin{axis}[grid=none,
axis lines=middle,
axis line style = very thick,
xmin=-8,xmax=8,
ymin=-8,ymax=8,
xtick={-8,-6,...,8},
ytick={-8,-6,...,8},
minor tick={},
xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(y\),
samples=250]
\addplot[domain=-7:7,thick] {6};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}

</solution>
</task>



<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
The line through <m>(1,3)</m> and <m>(-4,1)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The slope is 
<md>
m = \frac{1-3}{-4-1}  = \frac{-2}{-5}  = \frac{2}{5}.
</md> So the line is neither vertical nor horizontal.
</p>
</solution>
</task>



<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
The line through <m>(2,-5)</m> and <m>(2,4)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The slope is
<md>
m = \frac{4-(-5)}{2-2}  = \frac{9}{0} \qquad \text{which is undefined!}
</md> Therefore the line is vertical, and the <m>x</m>-intercept is <m>x=2</m>. The graph is below.
</p>


\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
\begin{axis}[grid=none,
axis lines=middle,
axis line style = very thick,
xmin=-3,xmax=3,
ymin=-3,ymax=3,
xtick={-3,-2,-1,1,2,3},
xticklabels={-3,-2,-1,1,2,3},
ytick={-3,-2,-1,1,2,3},
minor tick={},
xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(y\),
samples=250]
\draw[thick] (axis cs:2,3)--(axis cs:2,-3);
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}

</solution>
</task>


</exercise>